  "body": "### What is the issue with the HTML Standard?\n\nIt seems there's a gap between two parts of the spec regarding the expected behavior of `<dialog>` when it is within a hidden container.\n\n```html\n<div hidden><dialog id=\"modal\"></dialog></div>\n<script>document.getElementById(\"modal\").showModal();</script>\n```\nThe spec says that the `showModal` method in this situation should place the dialog in the top layer and make the page inert. However, the spec also says that a hidden element takes itself and all children out of the rendering tree.\n\nWhat happens in Chrome/Edge now is that the dialog element itself is not rendered, but the page is made inert.\n\nThat seems to be in line with the spec since [this part](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/interactive-elements.html#show-a-modal-dialog) never requires that a dialog element must be within a context that would normally allow it to be rendered.\n\nThis conflicts with an author's expectation, by placing the dialog in a context that is hidden, they expect that this part of the document is not rendered at all, including the dialog element within. Either that, or the dialog needs to actually be visible in this situation regardless of whether its ancestor is rendered, in line with setting the document inert.",
